# Declared network listeners — the allowlist for scripts/listener_audit.sh
#
# An ALLOWLIST, not a blocklist: anything listening on a non-loopback address
# that is not named here is reported. A blocklist silently exempts every
# service nobody thought of, which is exactly how an undeclared one survives.
#
# Format:  <scope>:<port>   # what it is, and why it is allowed to be reachable
#
# <scope> is a label, never an address. Addresses live in connections.md.
#   local  — the machine the practice root sits on
#   add your own labels for other hosts you audit (one per machine)
#
# Adding a line here is a decision that a service may be reached over the
# network. Make it deliberately: an entry is a standing grant, and the audit
# will never mention that port again.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS THE TEMPLATE. Copy it before use:
#
#   cp system/config/declared_listeners.txt.template \
#      system/config/declared_listeners.txt
#
# The working copy is gitignored, because which services a workshop runs is
# that workshop's business and does not belong in a public repository. Edit
# the copy; keep practice-level changes in this template. Ports ship as
# mechanism, never as one practice's data.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- local: macOS system services, present on any Mac ---
local:5000     # ControlCenter — AirPlay receiver (macOS built-in)
local:7000     # ControlCenter — AirPlay receiver (macOS built-in)

# --- your services: add below, one per line, each with a reason ---
# local:8080   # example — a local dev server you deliberately expose
#
# Before adding an entry, ask the three questions that make it safe to grant:
#   1. Does it need a non-loopback bind at all, or would 127.0.0.1 do?
#   2. Does it authenticate? A private network is a smaller set of readers,
#      not a trusted one.
#   3. If it serves files, is containment checked on the RESOLVED path?
#      A prefix check passes `desk/../../../.ssh/id_ed25519`.
#
# Note: rapportd (Apple Continuity) binds ephemeral high ports that change on
# every boot, so it cannot be pinned to a number. It is matched by process
# name in the script's SYSTEM_PROCS list, not by an entry here.
